  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nFolks, there is light at the end of the tunnel for this long term \nforecast discussion. The 90 degree temperatures may finally exit the \nCWA. This is not a drill. The current forecast package shows zero 90 \ndegree high temperature values on Thursday across north and central \nGeorgia, and there is pretty darn good ensemble agreement as to why \n(for now). We just got to make it through the next week.\n\nTrough moving through the NE this weekend won't bring a strong \nenough surface high to send any sort of relief our way just yet \n(foreshadowing!), and instead moist airmass will move overhead as \nthe TUTT become cut-off low off the Florida coastline moves to the \nwest. Expect a summertime like airmass, with highs in the 90s and \nafternoon and evening thunderstorm chances through the midweek. \nMonday and Tuesday currently look like the most favorable days for \nthe afternoon convection. Severe weather chances look low at this \ntime, mostly due to a lack of shear that should remain further to \nthe north.\n\nThings get a bit more interesting into the middle of the week. A \nbroad surface high will move across Canada trailing another \ntrough within the mid-latitude jet that has been shunted pretty \nfar poleward for September. This surface high moves into eastern \nmaritime Canadian provinces with broad reflection well into the NE\nCONUS into Wednesday. There is very broad ensemble agreement in \nthis, both in the EURO and GEFS, with GEFS showing an -average- \nhigh strength around 1035 mb in some of the recent runs, an \nimpressive signal 5-6 days out. This will set us up for a pretty \ndecent CAD event, with the wedge well south down the Appalachians.\nWhere a bit more uncertainty lies is in how the cut-off lows to \nthe west interact with all of this. Some model runs shows one as a\nshortwave to the north that helps aid the development of a \ncoastal low that enhances the wedge, with timing variable on when \nbetween Wednesday into Friday. Regardless of outcome, one result \nthat looks clear is that cooler, more Fall-like air should come \ninto the CWA some time next week. If the strongest of our wedge \ndreams come true, high temps could be as low as the 70s in some \nareas (looking at you 10th percentile), while 80s seem to be more \nfavored at the moment.\n\nLusk",
